---
scope: src/service/install/configureIde
status: aligned
description: "Wires every supported IDE so .aide files render with the editor's full Markdown experience — same syntax highlighting and same preview behavior as a .md file."
intent: >
  configureIde makes the .aide suffix visually first-class in every
  editor an adopter might use, so the convention reads as deliberate
  rather than as unstyled text. Each supported editor gets the
  approach its own extensibility model actually accommodates — the
  developer experience converges on full Markdown rendering even
  though the mechanism underneath diverges per editor. Per-editor
  shape is decided by what each editor's API can deliver, never by a
  cross-editor consistency goal that would force one of them into a
  half-working state.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every supported editor renders an .aide file with the same syntax highlighting and the same preview affordance the editor offers for a .md file. Partial highlighting without working preview is not success.
    - Each editor is wired through the mechanism its own extensibility model genuinely supports, even when that means different mechanisms across editors. The success criterion is rendering parity, not mechanism parity.
    - IDE wiring runs fully offline. No network call, marketplace round-trip, or build step is required at install time — whatever the editor needs ships with the package.
    - Existing user IDE configuration survives wiring untouched outside the keys this layer must change. A user's themes, keybindings, and unrelated language settings are never collateral damage.
    - An IDE the adopter does not use degrades to a skipped step rather than blocking the install. Missing tooling for one editor never aborts wiring for the others.
  undesired:
    - An editor wired through a mechanism its API cannot fully support, producing partial highlighting with a missing or broken preview. A half-working integration reads as a broken tool and is worse than skipping that editor entirely.
    - A cross-editor consistency goal that forces one editor into a shape its extensibility model does not accommodate. Per-editor strategy beats uniform shape whenever the editors' APIs genuinely diverge.
    - IDE wiring that downloads, builds, or fetches anything at install time. Network dependence at install is a regression — offline laptops must wire successfully.
    - An editor configurator that rewrites the user's IDE config from a template on parse failure or otherwise replaces keys outside its concern. Unparseable user config is a skip, never a clobber.
    - A missing editor or missing tooling that aborts the entire install rather than degrading the affected editor's wiring to skipped. Adopters who use only one editor must not be blocked by the absence of another.
---
